1
00:00:04,337 --> 00:00:05,379
{\an8}Wow.

2
00:00:09,175 --> 00:00:11,552
- That was exhausting.
- That was something, huh?

3
00:00:11,969 --> 00:00:14,555
- Good ride, Boyd, good ride.
- Oh, look at that!

4
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:16,182
- Oh, man.
- That is gorgeous.

5
00:00:16,307 --> 00:00:17,517
It's beautiful.

6
00:00:21,354 --> 00:00:23,564
- Well done, huh?
- Let's get a picture of the group.

7
00:00:23,689 --> 00:00:25,399
You know what?
That's great idea.

8
00:00:25,525 --> 00:00:26,567
- Um...
- Where do you wanna...

9
00:00:26,692 --> 00:00:27,818
- Right there. I think...
- Right here on the rock?

10
00:00:27,944 --> 00:00:28,986
- Right there.
- Now come over here.

11
00:00:29,111 --> 00:00:30,738
- Come on, everybody, let's line up.
- All right, line it up.

12
00:00:30,863 --> 00:00:32,990
Get tight, and we'll get
back on the road and ride some more.

13
00:00:33,115 --> 00:00:34,534
- All right, you ready?
- How's my hair?

14
00:00:34,659 --> 00:00:35,993
- All right.
- Okay, one more.

15
00:00:39,455 --> 00:00:41,123
- Got it. One more vertical.
- How's my hair look?

16
00:00:41,249 --> 00:00:42,375
All right.

17
00:00:43,459 --> 00:00:44,710
Get out of the way!

18
00:00:51,342 --> 00:00:52,677
What was that?

19
00:00:58,224 --> 00:01:00,309
- I didn't see anybody.
- Anybody moving down there?

20
00:01:00,726 --> 00:01:02,228
- That was...
- That was crazy!

21
00:01:02,645 --> 00:01:05,606
- He came out of nowhere.
- Right, did you see...

22
00:01:07,817 --> 00:01:08,985
Oh, my God!

23
00:01:10,111 --> 00:01:11,237
Somebody get help!

24
00:01:11,362 --> 00:01:13,072
- Who is this?
- Call 911!

25
00:01:13,406 --> 00:01:16,534
911! Okay. I got it! I got it!
I'm getting my phone!

26
00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:28,588
Help me.

27
00:01:30,590 --> 00:01:32,967
- Relax.
- Help me.

28
00:01:33,092 --> 00:01:35,052
It's gonna be fine.
You're gonna be okay.

29
00:01:36,512 --> 00:01:37,722
It's alright.

30
00:01:38,764 --> 00:01:39,849
Hang in there.

31
00:01:59,577 --> 00:02:00,995
I have a question.

32
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:03,623
Theoretically, if you wanted to break
into a maximum security prison

33
00:02:03,748 --> 00:02:05,082
how would you do it?

34
00:02:06,751 --> 00:02:08,836
You mean to get past
the razor wire perimeter fence

35
00:02:08,961 --> 00:02:11,964
the roaming patrols
closed-circuit camera surveillance?

36
00:02:12,423 --> 00:02:13,466
Yes.

37
00:02:14,425 --> 00:02:18,137
Theoretically, I don't think you can break
into a maximum security prison.

38
00:02:18,638 --> 00:02:20,556
Hmm. Hmm.

39
00:02:21,641 --> 00:02:23,726
I have plenty of other things
I could be doing, Lisbon.

40
00:02:23,851 --> 00:02:25,186
I don't know why you
brought me out here.

41
00:02:25,311 --> 00:02:27,229
It's good to get
out in the sunshine and the fresh air.

42
00:02:27,355 --> 00:02:30,149
You've been holed up for weeks
obsessing over Lorelei Martins.

43
00:02:30,274 --> 00:02:31,317
It's unhealthy.

44
00:02:31,442 --> 00:02:33,569
Lorelei Martins is the closest
we've ever gotten to Red John.

45
00:02:33,694 --> 00:02:35,863
It doesn't bother you that the FBI
just took her away from us?

46
00:02:35,988 --> 00:02:38,783
Of course it bothers me
but there's nothing I can do about it.

47
00:02:38,908 --> 00:02:41,369
- There's nothing you can do, either.
- How long is this gonna take?

48
00:02:41,494 --> 00:02:44,038
It's a jurisdictional thing.
It shouldn't take more than a couple hours.

49
00:02:44,163 --> 00:02:46,791
- Jurisdictional?
- Vehicular fatality. We're in a state park.

50
00:02:46,916 --> 00:02:49,001
Park rangers aren't equipped
to deal with this sort of thing.

51
00:02:49,126 --> 00:02:50,878
That's why we're here...
to do the paperwork.

52
00:02:51,003 --> 00:02:52,338
Excellent. Let's get on with it.

53
00:02:52,630 --> 00:02:53,673
Shall we?

54
00:03:04,725 --> 00:03:06,352
You guys must be CBI.

55
00:03:06,602 --> 00:03:08,145
It's about time. Sign here.

56
00:03:08,437 --> 00:03:09,605
All right.

57
00:03:16,987 --> 00:03:19,740
- Just my luck. Damn it!
- What?

58
00:03:20,449 --> 00:03:23,119
Get your yellow tape out.
It's a crime scene.

59
00:03:23,619 --> 00:03:24,662
Say what?

60
00:03:25,121 --> 00:03:27,790
It's a murder. Do whatever it is
you do when it's a murder.

61
00:03:28,124 --> 00:03:31,210
Murder? What?
No, it... it was an accident.

62
00:03:31,335 --> 00:03:33,754
Oh, really?
Why weren't there any skid marks?

63
00:03:35,131 --> 00:03:36,590
- Well, but...
- Damage to the guard rail

64
00:03:36,716 --> 00:03:38,926
indicates the car went straight
through it at a high rate of speed.

65
00:03:39,051 --> 00:03:41,303
Which means the driver
never engaged the brakes

66
00:03:41,429 --> 00:03:42,763
never touched
the steering wheel.

67
00:03:43,222 --> 00:03:44,849
So then it's a suicide.

68
00:03:44,974 --> 00:03:46,726
She was halfway
through a cheeseburger.

69
00:03:46,851 --> 00:03:49,478
She just had her car cleaned.
And where's her handbag?

70
00:03:49,812 --> 00:03:52,231
A woman like this would have a handbag
unless, of course, you have it.

71
00:03:52,356 --> 00:03:53,524
Do you have her handbag?

72
00:03:53,649 --> 00:03:55,443
- No.
- Then it was stolen.

73
00:03:55,735 --> 00:03:57,445
Probably by the same person
that pushed her off the road

74
00:03:57,570 --> 00:03:58,988
or tampered with her brakes.

75
00:03:59,113 --> 00:04:00,823
- Wow.
- Yes. Wow.

76
00:04:01,282 --> 00:04:02,700
My work is done here.

77
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:26,288
Forensics just confirmed it.

78
00:04:26,413 --> 00:04:27,998
No way those brake lines
failed on their own.

79
00:04:28,123 --> 00:04:29,708
- Definitely murder.
- Poor kid.

80
00:04:29,833 --> 00:04:33,295
- What have we got so far?
- Uh, the victim was Cassie Flood, 26.

81
00:04:33,420 --> 00:04:36,006
She worked for Channel 7
doing human interest stories.

82
00:04:36,131 --> 00:04:37,383
I recognize her.

83
00:04:37,508 --> 00:04:40,344
She did that story about the baby tiger
at the zoo, right?

84
00:04:40,469 --> 00:04:44,473
Probably. The last story she did was
on space travel for rich people.

85
00:04:45,766 --> 00:04:49,311
"And ordinary people like me will
be able to fly into space on this"

86
00:04:49,436 --> 00:04:51,563
"just walk up and buy a ticket?"

87
00:04:51,814 --> 00:04:53,941
"There are no ordinary people, Cassie."

88
00:04:54,483 --> 00:04:57,528
"We are all of us
extraordinary in our own way."

89
00:04:58,278 --> 00:05:00,197
"How much is it gonna cost
for me to get on this flight?"

90
00:05:00,322 --> 00:05:02,574
"First flight quarter of a million."

91
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:03,742
"Thereabouts."

92
00:05:03,867 --> 00:05:07,579
"Wow, that is quite an expense
for a 60-minute flight."

93
00:05:07,913 --> 00:05:09,748
"It's not the flight you pay for."

94
00:05:10,290 --> 00:05:12,960
- "It's the landing."
- "Oh. Wow. There you have it."

95
00:05:13,085 --> 00:05:16,213
{\an8}- "Now back to Ed Hunt..."
- Okay. Well, she's very telegenic.

96
00:05:16,755 --> 00:05:19,383
{\an8}She ever do any hard news? Something
that would have made her enemies?

97
00:05:19,508 --> 00:05:22,761
{\an8}Yeah, maybe. Couple months ago, she did
a piece on the Bells Glen Speedway.

98
00:05:23,178 --> 00:05:25,055
{\an8}Started as a human interest
story about stock cars

99
00:05:25,180 --> 00:05:28,016
{\an8}but ended up exposing a black market
of stolen cars and auto parts.

100
00:05:28,142 --> 00:05:31,103
{\an8}This guy, Dickie Yuntz
set to be indicted any day.

101
00:05:31,228 --> 00:05:32,771
{\an8}- You and Cho follow up.
- Okay.

102
00:05:32,896 --> 00:05:35,441
{\an8}Grace, this was originally
treated as an accident.

103
00:05:35,691 --> 00:05:37,901
{\an8}Re-interview all the witnesses
from the accident report.

104
00:05:38,026 --> 00:05:39,987
{\an8}See if anyone remembers
anything suspicious.

105
00:05:40,112 --> 00:05:41,363
{\an8}Will do, boss.

106
00:05:48,996 --> 00:05:51,206
{\an8}Lisbon. So it's murder, is it?

107
00:05:51,331 --> 00:05:53,876
It is. Let's go to the news
station the victim worked at.

108
00:05:54,001 --> 00:05:56,754
{\an8}Uh, I'm kind of busy.
Maybe later.

109
00:05:57,045 --> 00:05:59,715
{\an8}That's not how it works.
We have to do our jobs.

110
00:06:00,382 --> 00:06:02,885
{\an8}Well, I think you can do it.
You're very capable.

111
00:06:03,010 --> 00:06:04,803
I'm not kidding.
Come on, let's go.

112
00:06:04,928 --> 00:06:07,222
{\an8}Okay, I'll go
to the news station with you

113
00:06:07,347 --> 00:06:09,933
get you started, and then
you're on your own, all right?

114
00:06:10,058 --> 00:06:12,269
I am not in the sixth grade.
That doesn't work on me.

115
00:06:12,895 --> 00:06:15,689
{\an8}I can't promise I'll always
be around to help you, Teresa.

116
00:06:16,148 --> 00:06:19,359
{\an8}All right? There will come a time when
you might have to do all this on your own

117
00:06:20,027 --> 00:06:21,361
and I want you to be prepared.

118
00:06:21,487 --> 00:06:24,031
{\an8}I am prepared to punch you
in the face all on my own.

119
00:06:25,282 --> 00:06:27,117
{\an8}"And that's the weather. Ed?"

120
00:06:27,868 --> 00:06:31,955
"Next up... did the vice president
really say that?"

121
00:06:32,289 --> 00:06:33,665
"Right after the break."

122
00:06:37,461 --> 00:06:40,047
{\an8}Okay, cue up the Veep gaffe
for when we're back.

123
00:06:40,172 --> 00:06:41,381
{\an8}And...

124
00:06:42,591 --> 00:06:44,259
{\an8}Get ready to go to sports.

125
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:46,261
So, Mr. Kasmir

126
00:06:46,386 --> 00:06:48,472
{\an8}did Cassie ever have any
threats directed against her?

127
00:06:48,597 --> 00:06:50,933
{\an8}Maybe after the Bells Glen
Speedway story?

128
00:06:51,058 --> 00:06:52,351
Uh, the first note came
a week ago

129
00:06:52,476 --> 00:06:54,770
{\an8}saying Cassie should
leave town and not come back.

130
00:06:54,895 --> 00:06:56,605
{\an8}The second note arrived yesterday.

131
00:06:58,398 --> 00:07:01,026
{\an8}Uh, you didn't think this was
worth mentioning to the police?

132
00:07:01,151 --> 00:07:03,070
Look, we get crank letters
all the time.

133
00:07:03,779 --> 00:07:07,699
{\an8}We don't call the cops
every time one comes in.

134
00:07:07,950 --> 00:07:09,034
Go with this version.

135
00:07:10,702 --> 00:07:11,870
{\an8}Cassie was a valued person here.

136
00:07:11,995 --> 00:07:14,122
{\an8}She was a part of this team.
We cared about her.

137
00:07:14,248 --> 00:07:17,668
{\an8}So don't mistake our commitment
to the news for a lack of feeling.

138
00:07:17,793 --> 00:07:20,212
{\an8}Oh, we're not here to judge.
I promise you.

139
00:07:20,337 --> 00:07:22,923
{\an8}We're just trying to find
Cassie's killer, same as you.

140
00:07:24,383 --> 00:07:29,972
{\an8}You guys are great.
Oh, tough, uh, good-looking, sincere.

141
00:07:30,472 --> 00:07:32,683
{\an8}Let's put you on the air
ask the public for information

142
00:07:32,808 --> 00:07:35,561
{\an8}- you know, blah, blah, blah.
- That's not how we work.

143
00:07:35,686 --> 00:07:37,229
It could be good
for your investigation.

144
00:07:37,354 --> 00:07:40,607
Maybe later, if things
don't work out.

145
00:07:40,732 --> 00:07:42,651
If you were to use one word
to sum up Cassie

146
00:07:42,776 --> 00:07:44,695
- what would that word be?
- Promise.

147
00:07:44,987 --> 00:07:46,113
"Promise."

148
00:07:46,405 --> 00:07:48,115
- She had great promise.
- Great word.

149
00:07:48,240 --> 00:07:50,367
Just the other day
I was telling her she had, uh...

150
00:07:51,034 --> 00:07:52,411
the anchor chair in her future.

151
00:07:53,078 --> 00:07:54,288
Look, I don't wanna force
you to do anything

152
00:07:54,413 --> 00:07:55,831
but public attention is good.

153
00:07:56,081 --> 00:07:58,375
Bottom line... it could
help you solve the case.

154
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:01,545
- Did you, uh, get any more of these letters?
- I'll have to check with my intern

155
00:08:01,670 --> 00:08:03,213
but why don't I get you
on the air and we talk about it?

156
00:08:03,338 --> 00:08:05,674
- Mm, not gonna happen.
- You sure?

157
00:08:05,799 --> 00:08:08,051
Absolutely positive.
I don't belong on television.

158
00:08:12,848 --> 00:08:13,891
Excuse me.

159
00:08:15,017 --> 00:08:17,686
CBI. We're looking
for Richard Yuntz.

160
00:08:18,103 --> 00:08:19,354
Yeah.

161
00:08:23,108 --> 00:08:24,359
Over there.

162
00:08:24,484 --> 00:08:25,986
Police! Stop!

163
00:08:37,831 --> 00:08:38,874
Get up.

164
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:42,210
California Bureau of Investigation.

165
00:08:42,544 --> 00:08:44,338
- You're under arrest.
- All right, all right. Relax!

166
00:08:44,463 --> 00:08:46,131
Damn, I was just going
to take a leak!

167
00:08:56,558 --> 00:08:58,018
- Changes.
- Oh. Thanks.

168
00:09:02,564 --> 00:09:04,107
Where are my glasses?

169
00:09:04,775 --> 00:09:07,527
Tara, did you move my glasses?
I've told you before...

170
00:09:07,945 --> 00:09:10,864
- Do not touch them.
- No, Ed, I didn't touch your glasses.

171
00:09:10,989 --> 00:09:12,240
Well, where are they then?

172
00:09:12,366 --> 00:09:14,159
You lost them, Ed
like you always do

173
00:09:14,284 --> 00:09:15,869
and then you blamed me
like you always do

174
00:09:15,994 --> 00:09:20,082
and now I've called for the PA
to bring you another pair like I always do.

175
00:09:20,749 --> 00:09:23,126
Ooh. Thank you, Erin.

176
00:09:23,251 --> 00:09:26,546
Um, could you get me some
chamomile tea for my throat?

177
00:09:26,672 --> 00:09:28,674
- Sure.
- Thank you.

178
00:09:31,426 --> 00:09:35,013
Listen here, young woman.
I have three Emmy nominations.

179
00:09:35,138 --> 00:09:36,974
I've worked with Brokaw
and Blitzer.

180
00:09:37,432 --> 00:09:39,059
Show a little respect.

181
00:09:39,184 --> 00:09:41,895
Regional. Three regional
Emmy nominations.

182
00:09:44,523 --> 00:09:45,649
Regional.

183
00:09:51,029 --> 00:09:53,031
"I'm Ed Hunt..."

184
00:09:57,828 --> 00:10:00,789
Say, you're the lady from the television.

185
00:10:00,914 --> 00:10:03,041
- The... the... the weather, right?
- Yes.

186
00:10:03,166 --> 00:10:05,127
- Uh, Tara Skye.
- That's right, yeah.

187
00:10:05,252 --> 00:10:07,254
- Is that your real name?
- Yes.

188
00:10:07,379 --> 00:10:09,006
- That's very convenient.
- Um...

189
00:10:09,631 --> 00:10:11,925
What, uh, what can I do for you?
Autograph?

190
00:10:12,384 --> 00:10:15,595
No, no.
You probably heard about the murder.

191
00:10:15,971 --> 00:10:18,682
Cassie Flood, yeah.
Ugh, isn't it terrible?

192
00:10:19,099 --> 00:10:20,183
- Yeah.
- You with the cops?

193
00:10:20,308 --> 00:10:24,187
Well, I'm... I'm a consultant, but, uh...
you worked with Cassie, so...

194
00:10:24,312 --> 00:10:25,564
You may be able
to tell me the truth.

195
00:10:25,689 --> 00:10:28,316
You know, I've... I've asked
around about her

196
00:10:28,442 --> 00:10:31,236
but everyone seems to wanna
tell me just what they think they should tell

197
00:10:31,361 --> 00:10:32,404
you know what I mean?

198
00:10:32,529 --> 00:10:34,865
- Totally. What do you wanna know?
- Mm. Well...

199
00:10:36,033 --> 00:10:38,744
If you were to describe Cassie in one word
what would that word be?

200
00:10:40,996 --> 00:10:43,749
She was sleeping her way
to the top, if that helps.

201
00:10:44,041 --> 00:10:47,377
Well, that's a little more than one word
but I'm intrigued. Go on.

202
00:10:47,794 --> 00:10:51,506
The minute she got here
she jumped in the sack with Ed Hunt.

203
00:10:51,840 --> 00:10:54,885
And lately, she started
seducing Ryan Kasmir.

204
00:10:55,010 --> 00:10:58,388
But... I don't...
I don't wanna speak ill of the dead.

205
00:10:58,513 --> 00:11:00,057
They don't really care.
They're dead.

206
00:11:00,182 --> 00:11:02,434
Lisbon. Ah, there you are.

207
00:11:02,559 --> 00:11:04,644
Uh, I found a real journalist
to tell us the story.

208
00:11:05,395 --> 00:11:06,813
Tara, this is Teresa Lisbon.

209
00:11:06,938 --> 00:11:09,357
She works for the California
Bureau of Investigation.

210
00:11:09,483 --> 00:11:11,068
- Hi.
- Tara was just telling me

211
00:11:11,193 --> 00:11:14,029
- the truth about Cassie Flood.
- Really?

212
00:11:14,946 --> 00:11:16,031
The day she died

213
00:11:16,156 --> 00:11:19,242
I overheard her making plans to meet
with someone in secret.

214
00:11:19,701 --> 00:11:20,911
Really? Who was it?

215
00:11:22,037 --> 00:11:25,457
Did she tell you where she was going
or did you hear where she was going?

216
00:11:25,582 --> 00:11:27,501
No, but she was conniving

217
00:11:27,626 --> 00:11:30,879
and used her sexuality
to get ahead, so, you know...

218
00:11:31,004 --> 00:11:32,631
You think she was planning
to meet a secret lover?

219
00:11:32,756 --> 00:11:33,799
- Yeah.
- Sly minx.

220
00:11:33,924 --> 00:11:35,092
- I know, right?
- Whoa.

221
00:11:35,217 --> 00:11:37,552
Everyone treated her like
this perfect little angel.

222
00:11:37,761 --> 00:11:39,554
- That must have been very frustrating.
- Yeah.

223
00:11:39,679 --> 00:11:42,682
I had been here for 16 months
before she even showed up

224
00:11:42,808 --> 00:11:47,062
and in all that time Hunt and Kasmir
never even gave me the time of day.

225
00:11:47,187 --> 00:11:49,856
But her, with her east coast
journalism degree...

226
00:11:49,981 --> 00:11:52,484
I mean... they...
they were falling all over her.

227
00:11:52,609 --> 00:11:54,778
There she was
worrying about journalism

228
00:11:54,903 --> 00:11:56,696
while you were throwing yourself
at Hunt and Kasmir

229
00:11:56,822 --> 00:11:58,949
and she is the one that
ends up with the anchor chair.

230
00:11:59,074 --> 00:12:01,034
Yeah. I mean, no. I me...
what?

231
00:12:01,159 --> 00:12:04,454
Hell hath no fury like a slut outflanked.
You must have felt like killing her.

232
00:12:04,579 --> 00:12:05,622
- Yeah. W...
- Really?

233
00:12:05,747 --> 00:12:07,290
- Wait, no! No!
- No? Ow!

234
00:12:07,415 --> 00:12:08,792
How dare you?

235
00:12:12,003 --> 00:12:14,548
Well, she's a definite maybe.

236
00:12:14,673 --> 00:12:17,259
That should give you enough
to work with, though, don't you think?

237
00:12:17,384 --> 00:12:18,593
I hope that hurt.

238
00:12:20,637 --> 00:12:21,680
It did.

239
00:12:26,248 --> 00:12:30,002
Tara Skye resented Cassie Flood
both romantically and professionally.

240
00:12:30,127 --> 00:12:31,670
And she's got no alibi.

241
00:12:31,795 --> 00:12:35,090
The producer Kasmir says
he had no romantic relationship with Cassie

242
00:12:35,215 --> 00:12:37,468
and he says Tara Skye
is a backstabbing bitch.

243
00:12:37,593 --> 00:12:39,970
And he's got an alibi
for the night of the murder.

244
00:12:40,929 --> 00:12:43,140
What about Ed Hunt?

245
00:12:43,599 --> 00:12:44,767
- No alibi.
- Hmm.

246
00:12:44,892 --> 00:12:45,976
Says he was at home.

247
00:12:46,226 --> 00:12:50,272
Yuntz has motive and a weak alibi
plus an understanding of cars.

248
00:12:50,564 --> 00:12:52,941
What about the witnesses
from the accident site?

249
00:12:53,358 --> 00:12:54,568
Did they have anything useful?

250
00:12:54,693 --> 00:12:57,654
Well, they all mentioned the driver
who stopped and was the first to help

251
00:12:57,780 --> 00:12:59,323
and made sure the EMTs
were called.

252
00:12:59,448 --> 00:13:01,533
Everybody talked about him
but I could never find him.

253
00:13:01,658 --> 00:13:03,243
I've never been able
to speak to him directly.

254
00:13:03,368 --> 00:13:04,411
Did you get a description?

255
00:13:04,536 --> 00:13:07,331
Thirties, balding, white guy
average height, no plate.

256
00:13:07,456 --> 00:13:10,250
- I'm having a sketch done.
- We can try and get a public request.

257
00:13:10,375 --> 00:13:11,502
Maybe somebody will come in.

258
00:13:11,627 --> 00:13:13,879
In the meantime
I'm gonna go talk to Ed Hunt.

259
00:13:14,004 --> 00:13:15,047
All right.

260
00:13:22,471 --> 00:13:25,682
Hey, Lisbon. Uh, don't tell me
you've finished your case already.

261
00:13:26,100 --> 00:13:28,185
"We need to go and talk
to Ed Hunt. Where are you?"

262
00:13:28,310 --> 00:13:31,105
Uh, I'm not really
available at the moment

263
00:13:31,230 --> 00:13:33,565
but, uh, if you leave
a message after the tone...

264
00:13:33,690 --> 00:13:36,902
Cho told me you asked him
how to break into a high-security prison?

265
00:13:37,694 --> 00:13:42,282
Purely theoretical. And by the way
he was no help whatsoever.

266
00:13:42,533 --> 00:13:44,409
You're banging your head
against the wall.

267
00:13:45,410 --> 00:13:48,247
You know, I'll be back soon
so you can catch me up on your case then.

268
00:13:48,372 --> 00:13:49,581
I gotta go. Bye.

269
00:14:13,605 --> 00:14:14,773
I have a meeting with a client.

270
00:14:14,898 --> 00:14:16,608
I'm gonna have to ask you
to turn around, sir.

271
00:14:16,733 --> 00:14:17,985
But I have an appointment.

272
00:14:18,110 --> 00:14:19,862
No, sir, you do not
have an appointment.

273
00:14:20,320 --> 00:14:23,365
- You don't even know my name.
- Nobody has an appointment.

274
00:14:24,032 --> 00:14:25,450
This is a federal prison.

275
00:14:34,543 --> 00:14:37,880
We all know this money is
well pledged, don't we, folks?

276
00:14:38,338 --> 00:14:42,509
The environment, global warming...
these are good causes.

277
00:14:42,759 --> 00:14:45,554
And not just from the perspective
of beachfront property values.

278
00:14:46,013 --> 00:14:50,934
But our host and primary backer's
commitment to these causes is no joke.

279
00:14:51,226 --> 00:14:55,480
A man I have the honor
of calling a friend...

280
00:14:56,023 --> 00:14:57,191
Tommy Volker.

281
00:14:59,568 --> 00:15:02,196
But he's not just about saving the planet.

282
00:15:02,988 --> 00:15:07,618
There's also adventure
and, uh, plenty of the good things in life.

283
00:15:08,076 --> 00:15:12,706
But most importantly
there's this, ladies and gentlemen...

284
00:15:14,917 --> 00:15:20,464
A seat on the maiden flight
of the Volker Intergalactic

285
00:15:20,589 --> 00:15:24,593
the hardest ticket to get
in the whole world.

286
00:15:25,260 --> 00:15:28,347
Bidding starts at $200,000.

287
00:15:28,680 --> 00:15:31,892
And remember, you can write this off.

288
00:15:36,063 --> 00:15:37,147
Thank you.

289
00:15:37,648 --> 00:15:39,566
Thanks for being here.
Appreciate it.

290
00:15:40,359 --> 00:15:41,860
Thank you.
Thanks for being here.

291
00:15:41,985 --> 00:15:44,613
Uh, Mr. Hunt.
I'm Teresa Lisbon with the CBI.

292
00:15:44,738 --> 00:15:47,074
- Could we talk?
- Yeah, sure.

293
00:15:49,409 --> 00:15:53,247
Cassie was a great gal.
She was a real asset to the team.

294
00:15:55,249 --> 00:15:57,167
It was a tragedy to lose her.

295
00:15:57,292 --> 00:15:59,378
I was told you had a relationship with her.

296
00:15:59,503 --> 00:16:02,506
I have a relationship
with all my colleagues.

297
00:16:03,131 --> 00:16:05,342
But I'm... I'm guessing
that you mean something more.

298
00:16:05,467 --> 00:16:07,302
Yeah, I mean something more.

299
00:16:07,552 --> 00:16:10,013
Is that a common occurrence
with younger reporters

300
00:16:10,138 --> 00:16:12,182
or did you pick Cassie out specially?

301
00:16:12,307 --> 00:16:15,185
Tara Skye has been filling
your ear with poison, has she?

302
00:16:16,144 --> 00:16:19,439
The truth is, Cassie and I
were the best of friends.

303
00:16:19,564 --> 00:16:21,275
- Mm.
- But we were never exclusive.

304
00:16:21,566 --> 00:16:23,151
So you don't deny the affair?

305
00:16:23,402 --> 00:16:26,738
Oh, "affair"... that's...
that's an awful big word.

306
00:16:26,863 --> 00:16:28,407
We had a moment.

307
00:16:29,116 --> 00:16:32,035
Then we both moved on.
No hard feelings.

308
00:16:32,286 --> 00:16:34,830
I wonder if Cassie Flood would
agree with that description

309
00:16:34,955 --> 00:16:36,415
if she were here to answer.

310
00:16:36,623 --> 00:16:42,004
Here. Look. I...
got a picture of...

311
00:16:43,005 --> 00:16:46,133
us at the museum's opening
of the new Asian wing

312
00:16:46,425 --> 00:16:47,634
two weeks ago.

313
00:16:48,885 --> 00:16:50,846
Does that look like people
who don't get along?

314
00:16:51,138 --> 00:16:53,724
- Is that Tommy Volker?
- That's right.

315
00:16:53,849 --> 00:16:56,018
That was the night Cassie
convinced him to do the interview.

316
00:16:56,435 --> 00:16:58,812
Oh. Uh, how well do you
know Tommy Volker?

317
00:16:59,479 --> 00:17:00,856
We're old friends.

318
00:17:01,273 --> 00:17:04,776
We run in the same circles. Dakar, Baja.
We both like adventure...

319
00:17:05,068 --> 00:17:06,695
challenges, you know.

320
00:17:07,362 --> 00:17:08,864
Could... could you introduce me?

321
00:17:10,699 --> 00:17:12,075
Sure. Come on.

322
00:17:12,743 --> 00:17:14,286
I got people will say you told them

323
00:17:14,411 --> 00:17:17,789
you had plans to spit
on Cassie Flood's grave, Mr. Yuntz

324
00:17:18,790 --> 00:17:22,586
And yet the best alibi that you can offer
is that you were at the movies...

325
00:17:23,045 --> 00:17:24,463
by yourself.

326
00:17:24,588 --> 00:17:25,797
What can I tell you?

327
00:17:27,174 --> 00:17:29,885
I might have whacked her
if I knew what kind of story she was planning

328
00:17:30,010 --> 00:17:32,637
make me look so bad.
But I didn't.

329
00:17:33,722 --> 00:17:34,890
So I didn't.

330
00:17:35,724 --> 00:17:38,101
No point killing her
after the fact, right, Counselor?

331
00:17:38,226 --> 00:17:39,603
Indeed, no point.

332
00:17:39,895 --> 00:17:41,271
Uh, Agent Rigsby...

333
00:17:42,439 --> 00:17:44,399
If you believe and have evidence

334
00:17:44,524 --> 00:17:47,110
that my client has committed
a crime, please charge him.

335
00:17:47,235 --> 00:17:48,695
Otherwise, we're done.

336
00:17:50,364 --> 00:17:52,991
Yeah. We're done.

337
00:17:56,328 --> 00:17:58,121
- For now.
- Always a pleasure.

338
00:17:59,539 --> 00:18:01,041
Gotta get across town.

339
00:18:03,794 --> 00:18:05,670
Nice talking with you
Agent Bigsby.

340
00:18:09,674 --> 00:18:10,717
Thank you.

341
00:18:11,760 --> 00:18:13,178
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

342
00:18:14,888 --> 00:18:16,390
Hey. Tommy.

343
00:18:16,890 --> 00:18:18,266
Got someone I want you to meet.

344
00:18:18,767 --> 00:18:20,227
Agent Lisbon, Tommy Volker.

345
00:18:20,352 --> 00:18:22,396
Tommy, Teresa Lisbon.

346
00:18:23,605 --> 00:18:24,815
From the CBI.

347
00:18:26,608 --> 00:18:28,485
Oh. Would you excuse us
for a minute?

348
00:18:29,778 --> 00:18:33,740
Agent. It's a pleasure.
What brings you here?

349
00:18:34,157 --> 00:18:37,494
I'm looking into the death
of a young journalist, Cassie Flood.

350
00:18:37,619 --> 00:18:40,247
- She interviewed you.
- Uh, excuse me.

351
00:18:41,706 --> 00:18:46,753
Yeah. I heard about that. It's, uh...
it's a terrible thing. How can I help?

352
00:18:46,878 --> 00:18:49,923
Does anything come to mind that might
be useful for our investigation?

353
00:18:50,173 --> 00:18:53,343
She was, uh, poised
and charming for her age.

354
00:18:53,969 --> 00:18:57,055
And she had a wicked sense of humor
as well. I doubt that helps much.

355
00:18:57,931 --> 00:18:59,391
What a senseless tragedy.

356
00:18:59,891 --> 00:19:01,685
I'm sorry. Mr. Volker, we have to go.

357
00:19:01,810 --> 00:19:04,479
You're being conferenced into the
Mumbai board meeting in 20 minutes.

358
00:19:04,604 --> 00:19:06,148
So sorry about this, Agent Lisbon.

359
00:19:06,606 --> 00:19:09,901
If there's anything I can do
to help your investigation, I will. Um...

360
00:19:10,318 --> 00:19:14,406
Uh, here's my personal number.
Call me anytime.

361
00:19:16,366 --> 00:19:19,077
- And I mean that.
- Okay. Thanks.

362
00:19:28,086 --> 00:19:30,589
- Yuntz lawyered up?
- Yep. We're getting nowhere.

363
00:19:30,714 --> 00:19:32,215
Or maybe not.
Look at this.

364
00:19:34,134 --> 00:19:36,136
The Tide's Edge hotel. Huh.

365
00:19:37,554 --> 00:19:39,931
The only access is
from the road that Cassie was on.

366
00:19:41,766 --> 00:19:44,561
Not only that, Cassie Flood rented
a room on her credit card.

367
00:19:44,811 --> 00:19:47,606
When I called, front desk told me
Mr. Flood is still there.

368
00:19:47,731 --> 00:19:50,901
- She's single.
- Nice. I'll get Cho. We'll go pick him up.

369
00:19:51,026 --> 00:19:53,862
No need. CHP's bringing him in.
Should be here any minute.

370
00:19:59,034 --> 00:20:00,243
This is an outrage!

371
00:20:00,869 --> 00:20:03,538
Since when is California
a paramilitary state, huh?

372
00:20:04,206 --> 00:20:05,499
Ow! You're hurting me!

373
00:20:23,600 --> 00:20:24,643
Hey.

374
00:20:25,018 --> 00:20:27,562
Are you planning on coming
to work anytime soon?

375
00:20:27,687 --> 00:20:29,064
Well, yeah. Soonish.

376
00:20:30,482 --> 00:20:31,650
But, uh...

377
00:20:32,984 --> 00:20:34,069
Oop.

378
00:20:34,736 --> 00:20:37,447
I still got a few things
to, uh, figure out.

379
00:20:38,615 --> 00:20:39,991
Why, is there a break
in the case?

380
00:20:40,492 --> 00:20:43,370
- As a matter of fact, there is.
- Really? What?

381
00:20:43,620 --> 00:20:47,624
I've got somebody here who says Cassie Flood
was murdered by Tommy Volker.

382
00:20:56,464 --> 00:20:58,966
Mr. Berman.
You may take a seat.

383
00:20:59,842 --> 00:21:01,635
Or should I call you "Mr. Flood"?

384
00:21:03,471 --> 00:21:04,555
All right, look.

385
00:21:05,181 --> 00:21:09,018
Cassie Flood was gonna publish
a takedown piece in my magazine

386
00:21:09,143 --> 00:21:11,687
showing billionaire philanthropist
Tommy Volker

387
00:21:11,812 --> 00:21:14,815
to be a sleazy criminal
who just talks a good game.

388
00:21:14,940 --> 00:21:17,359
And Cassie Flood brought her story
to you why?

389
00:21:17,943 --> 00:21:19,904
Because of the wide readership
you receive?

390
00:21:20,029 --> 00:21:22,364
Cassie and I went to journalism
school together.

391
00:21:22,615 --> 00:21:26,035
And she knew that I publish a highly
respected investigative monthly.

392
00:21:26,160 --> 00:21:27,369
Opinions differ.

393
00:21:27,495 --> 00:21:30,706
Some think you publish a lefty rag
that sees conspiracies under every rock.

394
00:21:31,916 --> 00:21:33,334
I won a Polk award.

395
00:21:33,626 --> 00:21:35,503
Yeah. And you were sued for libel.

396
00:21:35,628 --> 00:21:38,672
Any good journalist gets sued.
It's part of the job.

397
00:21:39,673 --> 00:21:42,009
Cassie came to me because
she knew I'd have the courage

398
00:21:42,134 --> 00:21:44,428
to run her story
when nobody else would.

399
00:21:44,553 --> 00:21:45,596
Which was?

400
00:21:45,721 --> 00:21:48,766
Volker had a geothermal project
in the Amazon rain forest

401
00:21:48,891 --> 00:21:51,769
which was going to double
his presence in the emerging markets.

402
00:21:51,894 --> 00:21:54,355
But when an indigenous tribe
claimed the land sacred

403
00:21:54,480 --> 00:21:58,651
it threatened to kill his deal.
So he made them disappear.

404
00:21:59,568 --> 00:22:04,240
- The whole tribe?
- A whole village found in a mass grave.

405
00:22:05,366 --> 00:22:09,662
The killing was blamed on fighting between
drug traffickers and leftist rebels

406
00:22:09,787 --> 00:22:14,542
a terrible tragedy that just so happened
to remove all opposition to Volker's project.

407
00:22:14,667 --> 00:22:16,710
Oh, come on. Nobody could get
away with something like that.

408
00:22:16,836 --> 00:22:19,255
Volker did.
Look, he's smart.

409
00:22:20,005 --> 00:22:22,049
Does anything possible
to keep his hands clean.

410
00:22:22,174 --> 00:22:24,135
He'll get other people
to do his dirty work.

411
00:22:24,260 --> 00:22:26,554
- But Cassie... she caught him anyway.
- How?

412
00:22:27,179 --> 00:22:29,598
She had an inside source
in Volker's organization.

413
00:22:29,890 --> 00:22:31,559
That source gave her
audit trails.

414
00:22:32,017 --> 00:22:33,435
Audit trails never lie.

415
00:22:33,853 --> 00:22:36,647
Cassie was bringing me
the hard evidence when she died.

416
00:22:36,772 --> 00:22:37,815
Audit trails?

417
00:22:37,940 --> 00:22:40,067
You follow the money
you always get to the truth.

418
00:22:40,526 --> 00:22:43,445
Cassie was a top-notch
investigative journalist.

419
00:22:44,780 --> 00:22:46,448
She was wasted on television.

420
00:22:46,949 --> 00:22:48,409
Hence the missing bag.

421
00:22:48,659 --> 00:22:50,828
Grace's good Samaritan
was there to get the evidence.

422
00:22:50,953 --> 00:22:52,830
Show Volker this sketch
and watch his face.

423
00:22:53,330 --> 00:22:55,082
- I know what to do.
- I know you do.

424
00:22:55,416 --> 00:22:56,750
You go, girl. Huh?

425
00:23:14,977 --> 00:23:16,020
Thank you.

426
00:23:16,979 --> 00:23:19,481
Agent Lisbon. Is there something
wrong? What's the matter?

427
00:23:19,607 --> 00:23:20,816
I'm sorry to blind-side you

428
00:23:20,941 --> 00:23:23,777
but I have some questions I need to ask you
if you don't mind.

429
00:23:23,903 --> 00:23:25,738
- Mr. Volker's flying to New York.
- Of course.

430
00:23:25,863 --> 00:23:28,073
As I said before
whatever I can do to help.

431
00:23:28,490 --> 00:23:31,702
The thing is, we found out
where Cassie was going the day she died.

432
00:23:31,827 --> 00:23:32,953
Really? Where?

433
00:23:33,078 --> 00:23:34,872
She was going to meet a man
named Steve Berman.

434
00:23:35,372 --> 00:23:37,416
- And you think this Berman killed her?
- We don't know

435
00:23:37,541 --> 00:23:40,794
but he told us some pretty strange things
I wanted to run by you.

436
00:23:41,420 --> 00:23:42,463
All right.

437
00:23:42,963 --> 00:23:45,257
Did Cassie ever ask you
about the Amazon rain forest

438
00:23:45,382 --> 00:23:49,094
- when you and she spoke?
- The Amazon rain forest? No.

439
00:23:49,220 --> 00:23:52,139
Did she ever mention a geothermal project
that you have down there?

440
00:23:52,973 --> 00:23:54,266
Good Lord, no.

441
00:23:55,434 --> 00:23:58,646
Cassie and I talked about my...
self-consciousness

442
00:23:58,771 --> 00:24:00,231
growing up with a stutter

443
00:24:00,481 --> 00:24:02,566
and my feeling that I needed
to give something back

444
00:24:02,691 --> 00:24:04,151
because of all I received.

445
00:24:04,610 --> 00:24:05,694
That's all?

446
00:24:05,819 --> 00:24:08,989
No, we also bonded over having both
been raised by our grandmothers

447
00:24:09,114 --> 00:24:11,492
and we also swore
that we'd both remain committed

448
00:24:11,617 --> 00:24:14,453
to making the world a better place
for our having been in it.

449
00:24:14,578 --> 00:24:15,996
Amanda, you were there.

450
00:24:16,121 --> 00:24:18,749
Did, uh, did our
Amazon project come up?

451
00:24:18,874 --> 00:24:22,002
No. The... the Amazon
was never mentioned.

452
00:24:24,630 --> 00:24:27,508
She never mentioned
a village being massacred?

453
00:24:27,800 --> 00:24:29,218
Good Lord, no.

454
00:24:29,635 --> 00:24:32,930
What a bizarre question.
What village? What massacre?

455
00:24:33,055 --> 00:24:34,556
I'm just following a lead, sir.

456
00:24:36,350 --> 00:24:39,061
You know, perhaps I should
give you an alibi.

457
00:24:39,728 --> 00:24:44,149
It's all right. I wouldn't expect a man in
your position to do the dirty work himself.

458
00:24:47,611 --> 00:24:48,654
I'm sorry.

459
00:24:52,783 --> 00:24:54,868
No offense. Cop humor.

460
00:24:55,661 --> 00:24:57,413
By the way...

461
00:24:58,372 --> 00:24:59,748
Do you recognize this man?

462
00:24:59,957 --> 00:25:01,250
No. Why?

463
00:25:01,542 --> 00:25:03,419
Just following a lead, like I said.

464
00:25:03,711 --> 00:25:06,213
Agent, I know you're under a lot
of pressure right now.

465
00:25:06,714 --> 00:25:08,674
People want you to solve
this case yesterday.

466
00:25:08,966 --> 00:25:11,302
- I know what that's like.
- I appreciate that.

467
00:25:11,760 --> 00:25:13,262
So I don't want you to worry

468
00:25:14,221 --> 00:25:15,848
that you've offended me
in any way.

469
00:25:17,433 --> 00:25:20,436
- You don't, do you?
- No, if you say I haven't.

470
00:25:21,937 --> 00:25:23,147
Well, that's a relief.

471
00:25:24,315 --> 00:25:25,733
Now I really must be going.

472
00:25:26,358 --> 00:25:27,735
Amanda, call me from the office.

473
00:25:27,860 --> 00:25:30,404
There's some things
I need done before I land.

474
00:25:32,573 --> 00:25:34,408
- We'll talk again, I'm sure.
- Mm.

475
00:25:37,077 --> 00:25:40,164
Please, feel free to drop by anytime.

476
00:25:50,007 --> 00:25:51,550
Lisbon, what gives?

477
00:25:52,676 --> 00:25:54,345
This has gone on too long.

478
00:25:55,304 --> 00:25:57,014
I need you to accept the fact

479
00:25:57,139 --> 00:25:59,683
that you cannot break
Lorelei Martins out of prison.

480
00:25:59,808 --> 00:26:02,186
Yes, I know that now.
Thank you. You were right.

481
00:26:02,686 --> 00:26:05,606
You've come up with some plan
you don't want me to know about.

482
00:26:05,731 --> 00:26:06,940
Well, that's an interesting theory.

483
00:26:07,066 --> 00:26:09,943
I can see by your face
that Volker yielded nothing.

484
00:26:10,611 --> 00:26:12,696
But you still like him
for the murder.

485
00:26:12,821 --> 00:26:14,531
Yes, but he's tight
as a drum.

486
00:26:14,782 --> 00:26:16,950
His assistant's looking
very promising.

487
00:26:17,076 --> 00:26:19,161
I'm gonna go down there
and try to talk to her alone.

488
00:26:19,286 --> 00:26:22,289
- You want me to come with you?
- Don't bother. I can manage.

489
00:26:22,414 --> 00:26:24,249
You haven't really worked
on this case anyhow.

490
00:26:25,125 --> 00:26:26,251
Okay.

491
00:26:26,960 --> 00:26:30,714
- Uh, then... why are you here?
- Why are you here?

492
00:26:31,173 --> 00:26:33,675
Repetition is the lowest form
of repartee.

493
00:26:33,801 --> 00:26:35,594
Please, we can do
a little better than that.

494
00:26:37,388 --> 00:26:40,682
Okay, Lisbon, I will
get on the case. I promise.

495
00:26:40,808 --> 00:26:41,892
See you later.

496
00:26:57,113 --> 00:26:59,657
Amanda, it's Agent Lisbon.
I need to speak to you.

497
00:26:59,782 --> 00:27:01,367
I don't think
that's a good idea.

498
00:27:01,492 --> 00:27:02,743
"Amanda, listen to me."

499
00:27:03,369 --> 00:27:06,497
I know you think that Volker
killed Cassie Flood.

500
00:27:06,622 --> 00:27:09,291
I think you're very afraid right now.
I can help you.

501
00:27:12,837 --> 00:27:15,673
You can't say things like that.
That's not true.

502
00:27:17,383 --> 00:27:19,135
You could get me in serious trouble.

503
00:27:19,593 --> 00:27:21,053
Why, if it's not true?

504
00:27:21,679 --> 00:27:24,682
Look, Volker was in San Francisco
at a charity event

505
00:27:24,807 --> 00:27:26,892
with 300, 400 other people.

506
00:27:27,184 --> 00:27:28,227
I was there with him.

507
00:27:28,602 --> 00:27:30,020
He couldn't have killed
Cassie Flood.

508
00:27:30,146 --> 00:27:32,565
I think we both know he wouldn't
have killed her himself.

509
00:27:32,690 --> 00:27:34,400
No, I don't know that.

510
00:27:35,568 --> 00:27:39,280
Amanda, if you keep silent
you are just as guilty as he is.

511
00:27:39,447 --> 00:27:41,824
It's the same as if you
killed those people yourself...

512
00:27:41,949 --> 00:27:45,786
not just Cassie Flood, but every
person in that Amazon village...

513
00:27:46,579 --> 00:27:48,497
Women, children, babies.

514
00:27:59,133 --> 00:28:00,634
You don't know what he's like.

515
00:28:03,095 --> 00:28:04,847
What he's capable of.

516
00:28:04,972 --> 00:28:09,185
Amanda, I promise nothing
is gonna happen to you

517
00:28:09,310 --> 00:28:10,978
as long as you do the right thing.

518
00:28:11,479 --> 00:28:12,521
I don't know.

519
00:28:12,771 --> 00:28:16,442
You don't want this on your
conscience... any of it. You can't.

520
00:28:23,199 --> 00:28:25,284
After the TV piece...

521
00:28:27,745 --> 00:28:30,164
she came back, and she was
asking questions

522
00:28:30,289 --> 00:28:33,959
about the geothermal project
and the massacred village.

523
00:28:34,460 --> 00:28:36,337
And he told her that
the stories weren't true.

524
00:28:37,338 --> 00:28:41,300
But I could see
that he was angry and lying.

525
00:28:45,179 --> 00:28:46,472
That's all I know.

526
00:28:47,306 --> 00:28:49,433
It's not enough to prove anything, is it?

527
00:28:50,309 --> 00:28:52,311
No. No, but it helps.

528
00:28:52,645 --> 00:28:55,147
Thank you, Amanda.
You are doing the right thing.

529
00:28:57,775 --> 00:28:59,151
One last time.

530
00:29:01,070 --> 00:29:02,571
Do you know who this man is?

531
00:29:03,405 --> 00:29:04,448
No.

532
00:29:06,909 --> 00:29:09,662
- Who is he?
- We're still trying to figure that out.

533
00:29:11,914 --> 00:29:13,123
What will I do now?

534
00:29:13,749 --> 00:29:17,169
Nothing. After we arrest Volker
you're gonna need to come in

535
00:29:17,294 --> 00:29:19,046
and testify as to what you heard.

536
00:29:19,547 --> 00:29:21,715
Until then, just keep quiet.

537
00:29:21,840 --> 00:29:24,843
Carry on as normal.
Let us take care of everything.

538
00:29:27,304 --> 00:29:28,514
I'm scared.

539
00:29:29,848 --> 00:29:31,100
Do not worry.

540
00:29:32,309 --> 00:29:35,020
When it's time for you
to speak, we'll protect you.

541
00:29:35,896 --> 00:29:37,147
You have my word.

542
00:29:44,738 --> 00:29:46,156
"Sounds like it's gonna be a hot one."

543
00:29:46,282 --> 00:29:48,284
"You're not wrong there, Ed
but beautiful."

544
00:29:48,534 --> 00:29:49,577
"Anybody out there who can"

545
00:29:49,702 --> 00:29:52,413
{\an8}"it'd be a great time to visit
the mountains or the beach."

546
00:29:53,455 --> 00:29:56,333
{\an8}"Not so easy if you're
a working man, Tara."

547
00:29:56,750 --> 00:29:58,377
"Or a woman, Ed."

548
00:29:59,336 --> 00:30:00,504
"Right."

549
00:30:04,508 --> 00:30:07,011
Hello, Lisbon.
How do you do?

550
00:30:07,428 --> 00:30:09,221
She cracked.
Volker's our man.

551
00:30:09,346 --> 00:30:10,848
She doesn't have enough
to press charges on him.

552
00:30:10,973 --> 00:30:12,349
We still need the actual killer.

553
00:30:12,474 --> 00:30:14,893
Hmm. Funny you say that.
I just had an idea.

554
00:30:15,185 --> 00:30:16,395
What kind of idea?

555
00:30:16,520 --> 00:30:18,105
Come by the newsroom
tomorrow, and I'll show you.

556
00:30:18,230 --> 00:30:19,732
Oh, and... and bring Cho.

557
00:30:19,857 --> 00:30:22,610
- Good night.
- "Wait, what kind of idea?"

558
00:30:22,735 --> 00:30:23,861
Jane...

559
00:30:33,954 --> 00:30:35,539
Hello, Lisbon.
You at the newsroom?

560
00:30:35,664 --> 00:30:37,708
- Yeah. Where are you?
- I'll be there in a minute.

561
00:30:37,833 --> 00:30:39,793
I need to see an optometrist.

562
00:30:40,586 --> 00:30:42,921
- What?
- I'll be with you soon.

563
00:30:47,217 --> 00:30:49,511
- You're doing what?
- I'm going on the air.

564
00:30:49,637 --> 00:30:52,389
Mr. Kasmir convinced me
that it's the right thing to do.

565
00:30:52,514 --> 00:30:54,016
Ed, wind it up
and go to commercial.

566
00:30:54,308 --> 00:30:56,685
"We'll be right back
after this."

567
00:30:56,810 --> 00:30:59,271
Okay, but I'm gonna have to look
at the list of questions first.

568
00:30:59,396 --> 00:31:01,982
No need to worry about that, Lisbon.
I've taken care of that already.

569
00:31:02,107 --> 00:31:03,776
Ed, when we come back
from the commercial break

570
00:31:03,901 --> 00:31:06,403
we'll go to an update on the progress
in the Cassidy Flood murder case.

571
00:31:06,528 --> 00:31:09,031
"Okay. Is Mr. Jane here?"

572
00:31:09,657 --> 00:31:11,575
Coming right in. Okay.
Come on.

573
00:31:11,825 --> 00:31:14,536
- Showtime.
- Let's go get you prepped.

574
00:31:14,912 --> 00:31:16,955
Give him some lipstick
and some blush.

575
00:31:17,081 --> 00:31:18,624
Tara, did you move my glasses?

576
00:31:21,210 --> 00:31:23,337
- Tara?
- No.

577
00:31:23,796 --> 00:31:25,214
- Tara. Earth to Tara.
- No.

578
00:31:25,339 --> 00:31:28,550
- Did you move my glasses?
- Nope. Nope.

579
00:31:38,018 --> 00:31:40,479
Okay. All right. Okay.

580
00:31:40,646 --> 00:31:45,192
I will lay out the progress of the case
thus far and then I'll go to you.

581
00:31:45,317 --> 00:31:48,195
All right, Ed, and I'll bring it home
by telling you how we caught the killer.

582
00:31:50,072 --> 00:31:52,241
- You caught the killer?
- Yeah.

583
00:31:52,533 --> 00:31:55,494
Well, that's...
that's great. Who is it?

584
00:31:56,161 --> 00:32:00,249
Wh... what... what was it
that, uh... that led you to him?

585
00:32:00,499 --> 00:32:02,626
- It was all about the car in the end.
- The car?

586
00:32:03,460 --> 00:32:04,503
News to me.

587
00:32:04,628 --> 00:32:06,422
So we're just gonna
blow off the weather?

588
00:32:10,843 --> 00:32:14,972
And we're live
in three, two, one.

589
00:32:17,883 --> 00:32:19,843
{\an8}"This is Ed Hunt"

590
00:32:19,882 --> 00:32:23,302
with a special report
on the Cassie Flood murder case.

591
00:32:23,428 --> 00:32:28,391
Patrick Jane is a consultant
with the California Bureau of Investigation.

592
00:32:28,724 --> 00:32:30,268
- Hi, Ed.
- Mr. Jane.

593
00:32:30,393 --> 00:32:33,729
You've got shocking new information
in the Cassie Flood murder.

594
00:32:33,855 --> 00:32:35,940
- Is that right?
- Yes, I do, Ed.

595
00:32:36,524 --> 00:32:40,736
Now, Cassie's car was tampered with
but earlier here at the station

596
00:32:40,862 --> 00:32:44,740
we received a note that was connected
to a story of Cassie's

597
00:32:44,866 --> 00:32:47,493
about the Bells Glen Speedway.

598
00:32:47,618 --> 00:32:49,287
Are these things connected?

599
00:32:49,412 --> 00:32:52,373
Well, that was an early
avenue of the investigation.

600
00:32:52,498 --> 00:32:53,749
You're right there, Ed.

601
00:32:53,875 --> 00:32:56,502
"But it turns out that's not
what I'm here to talk about."

602
00:32:58,504 --> 00:32:59,547
Um...

603
00:32:59,672 --> 00:33:01,757
- You're... you're not?
- No.

604
00:33:01,883 --> 00:33:03,134
What the hell is he doing?

605
00:33:03,634 --> 00:33:06,179
- Is he going off script?
- Yeah. He does that.

606
00:33:06,596 --> 00:33:10,183
Uh, Tara, tell me
do you know Tommy Volker?

607
00:33:10,808 --> 00:33:12,727
Uh, no.

608
00:33:12,852 --> 00:33:15,396
- I... I mean, I know of him.
- Right.

609
00:33:15,521 --> 00:33:17,607
Uh, Ed, you know
Tommy Volker, don't you?

610
00:33:17,732 --> 00:33:21,027
Agent Jane, you were going to tell us
about the Bells Glen Speedway.

611
00:33:21,527 --> 00:33:25,323
Cassie Flood told you she wanted to do
a story on Tommy Volker

612
00:33:25,448 --> 00:33:27,825
and the massacred village
in the Amazon, right?

613
00:33:27,950 --> 00:33:30,578
Ok... okay, let's, uh...
let's cut this.

614
00:33:30,870 --> 00:33:33,748
This is great television.
Keep rolling until I say "stop."

615
00:33:34,248 --> 00:33:36,542
Ed, you... you and Tommy are old
acquaintances, aren't you?

616
00:33:36,667 --> 00:33:38,753
You do these adventures
together, like the...

617
00:33:38,878 --> 00:33:41,255
The Dakar rally.
You're... you're adventurers.

618
00:33:41,380 --> 00:33:43,341
- Right?
- Mr. Jane, um...

619
00:33:43,758 --> 00:33:45,635
These are not the questions
we discussed.

620
00:33:45,760 --> 00:33:48,304
The Dakar rally
is an endurance car race.

621
00:33:48,429 --> 00:33:50,681
You and Mr. Volker must know
a lot about cars.

622
00:33:50,932 --> 00:33:53,059
All right, we've... had enough.

623
00:33:53,601 --> 00:33:56,562
Do you deny that you competed in
the Dakar rally with Mr. Volker?

624
00:33:56,687 --> 00:33:59,565
- Of course not.
- And you don't deny having the, uh

625
00:33:59,690 --> 00:34:03,027
necessary knowledge to tamper
with Cassie Flood's car?

626
00:34:03,152 --> 00:34:04,654
- Yes!
- Yes.

627
00:34:04,779 --> 00:34:06,322
No. No!

628
00:34:08,449 --> 00:34:10,701
Do you deny that these
are your glasses?

629
00:34:12,078 --> 00:34:13,663
Uh, well, they... they do...

630
00:34:13,871 --> 00:34:16,207
- They look like mine.
- Mm. Uh-huh.

631
00:34:16,749 --> 00:34:19,001
We found them in the wreckage
of Cassie's car.

632
00:34:19,293 --> 00:34:21,671
Well, that's impossible.
I wasn't wearing them.

633
00:34:32,265 --> 00:34:33,307
I...

634
00:34:33,558 --> 00:34:34,725
I did not...

635
00:34:35,977 --> 00:34:37,812
leave those in her car.

636
00:34:38,104 --> 00:34:41,482
But you do admit to tampering
with Cassie Flood's car?

637
00:34:43,150 --> 00:34:44,193
No.

638
00:34:47,989 --> 00:34:50,116
{\an8}"As God is my witness, no."

639
00:34:55,413 --> 00:34:59,041
I think that counts
as an admission of guilt. Don't you?

640
00:35:01,419 --> 00:35:02,545
Go get him, Cho.

641
00:35:07,425 --> 00:35:10,636
{\an8}"We'll be right back
after this short... commercial break."

642
00:35:13,723 --> 00:35:16,684
I had no idea she'd be driving
on a road like that.

643
00:35:16,809 --> 00:35:19,395
You've gotta believe that.
I only meant to scare her.

644
00:35:19,520 --> 00:35:22,273
Just... to get her off the air.

645
00:35:22,773 --> 00:35:24,692
That's why you sent the notes
to make her leave town?

646
00:35:25,526 --> 00:35:27,987
I thought if she believed
Yuntz was out to get her, she'd...

647
00:35:28,571 --> 00:35:32,074
leave the station, go someplace else
take somebody else's job.

648
00:35:34,368 --> 00:35:36,621
- Not mine.
- Instead, you ended up killing her.

649
00:35:38,956 --> 00:35:41,417
It wasn't my idea.

650
00:35:42,293 --> 00:35:43,878
Volker...

651
00:35:45,171 --> 00:35:47,590
- put me up to it.
- He had you tinker with her car?

652
00:35:50,551 --> 00:35:54,388
We used to, uh, joke about
doing it during the Dakar...

653
00:35:55,222 --> 00:35:57,767
uh, as a prank on somebody
you know.

654
00:35:57,892 --> 00:36:00,102
So Volker told you to use it
to take care of Cassie.

655
00:36:02,647 --> 00:36:03,981
It was more of a dare.

656
00:36:04,607 --> 00:36:06,942
I couldn't back down
without looking like a coward.

657
00:36:07,068 --> 00:36:10,446
A dare? That was enough to convince you
to murder Cassie Flood?

658
00:36:10,571 --> 00:36:12,615
It wasn't supposed to be
a murder!

659
00:36:15,993 --> 00:36:17,536
So you were manipulated
into committing a murder

660
00:36:17,662 --> 00:36:19,246
beneficial to Tommy Volker.

661
00:36:19,372 --> 00:36:21,624
And readily admit
that you have no evidence

662
00:36:21,749 --> 00:36:23,542
that could implicate him
at all... is that about right?

663
00:36:27,338 --> 00:36:28,964
This guy's a fool.

664
00:36:29,965 --> 00:36:31,884
But this Volker seems formidable.

665
00:36:32,301 --> 00:36:34,387
As smart and as careful
as I thought.

666
00:36:34,512 --> 00:36:36,806
I don't know. How could
he be sure she'd be killed?

667
00:36:36,931 --> 00:36:38,224
It looks like he just got lucky.

668
00:36:38,516 --> 00:36:40,935
No, no. He always had
a plan "B."

669
00:36:42,144 --> 00:36:44,480
The good Samaritan wasn't
just there to get the evidence.

670
00:36:44,772 --> 00:36:46,691
He also made sure she was dead.

671
00:36:48,818 --> 00:36:50,736
Volker's not gonna
get away with this.

672
00:36:50,986 --> 00:36:52,279
I am gonna get him.

673
00:36:52,405 --> 00:36:55,199
If not for the murder, then whatever
Amanda Shaw can give me.

674
00:36:55,324 --> 00:36:57,702
Tax evasion, fraud
unpaid parking tickets.

675
00:36:57,827 --> 00:37:00,037
I... I'm gonna bring in Amanda
and lean on her.

676
00:37:00,162 --> 00:37:01,247
Look at you!

677
00:37:01,747 --> 00:37:02,832
So tough.

678
00:37:03,666 --> 00:37:05,126
I'm getting goosebumps.

679
00:37:32,111 --> 00:37:34,613
It's been ten hours since anybody's heard
from Amanda Shaw.

680
00:37:34,739 --> 00:37:36,991
We need a warrant to get
into her apartment right away.

681
00:37:39,326 --> 00:37:41,078
Thanks, Judge.
Let's go.

682
00:37:47,042 --> 00:37:48,169
Damn it.

683
00:37:54,800 --> 00:37:56,260
Let's get her down from there.

684
00:38:30,878 --> 00:38:32,797
She left a suicide note, you know?

685
00:38:34,715 --> 00:38:36,008
Via e-mail.

686
00:38:39,136 --> 00:38:40,888
Laying blame at your feet.

687
00:38:42,473 --> 00:38:45,100
You will not get away
with this. I promise.

688
00:38:51,941 --> 00:38:54,235
You have no idea what's
happening at all, do you?

689
00:38:58,823 --> 00:39:01,075
Enjoy your job while you
still have it, Teresa.

690
00:39:30,646 --> 00:39:33,023
- Agent "Lis-bon."
- It... it's Lisbon.

691
00:39:33,148 --> 00:39:35,067
Lisbon. Pardon me.

692
00:39:36,360 --> 00:39:38,612
Bob Kirkland
Homeland Security.

693
00:39:39,697 --> 00:39:41,949
We don't know each other
but we need to talk.

694
00:39:42,408 --> 00:39:45,160
- How can I help you, Mr. Kirkland?
- Call me Bob.

695
00:39:47,121 --> 00:39:49,498
- Okay. What is it?
- Volker.

696
00:39:50,708 --> 00:39:53,002
You need to stop worrying
about him, okay?

697
00:39:53,294 --> 00:39:57,089
"Stop worrying about him."
What is that supposed to mean?

698
00:39:59,383 --> 00:40:03,220
I'm telling you off the record
that it's being taken care of, trust me.

699
00:40:03,679 --> 00:40:05,097
You should take a step back.

700
00:40:05,222 --> 00:40:07,850
So you can take credit
for the arrest? Not a chance.

701
00:40:08,642 --> 00:40:11,186
No. No, it's not like that.

702
00:40:11,687 --> 00:40:12,897
But Volker is...

703
00:40:13,564 --> 00:40:15,900
He's a very powerful man...

704
00:40:16,942 --> 00:40:18,193
Agent.

705
00:40:19,528 --> 00:40:22,281
Big contributor
to a lot of campaigns.

706
00:40:23,282 --> 00:40:24,867
Is that supposed to scare me?

707
00:40:28,037 --> 00:40:29,079
No.

708
00:40:29,955 --> 00:40:31,832
You just need to watch your step.

709
00:40:35,502 --> 00:40:37,421
Can I buy you
a cup of coffee sometime?

710
00:40:40,007 --> 00:40:42,259
- Sure.
- Okay.

711
00:40:54,313 --> 00:40:55,814
- Sorry, Mr. Jane.
- Oh, excuse me.

712
00:40:55,940 --> 00:40:56,982
Do I know you?

713
00:40:58,400 --> 00:40:59,443
No.

714
00:41:00,486 --> 00:41:01,946
But I know you.
